  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 244 AM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Beneficial rain showers will continue across the North \nCountry through the weekend. A cold core upper level low will be \nsituated overhead which will be supportive of steep low to mid-level \nlapse rates which will help drive additional shower activity this \nweekend. The 850 mb temperature overhead will hover around 0 to -1 C \nover the weekend which supports rain showers anywhere below 3000 ft. \nIt's possible the summits pick up some minor snow accumulation \nthrough the weekend but will likely top out at an inch or two if \nthey are lucky. A more widespread rain event is expected once again \non Monday as a low pressure system tracks from the Mississippi River \nValley up through western New York. This, again, is another warm \nsolution with widespread rain expected through much of the day \nMonday. Rainfall totals won't be anything too impressive with \ngenerally a third to two thirds of an inch of rain expected through \nthe duration of the event. Cold air is expected to wrap around the \nlow pressure system Monday night but the cold air will likely lag \nthe main moisture so any snow will likely be limited to upslope \nshowers on the back end of the system.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Temperatures are favored to cool sharply Tuesday\nwith northwesterly flow advecting cold air into the region.\nSpeeds will be down from Monday, fortunately, but still feeling\nquite brisk for the morning commute with apparent temperatures\nin the upper teens to around 20 degrees. Those outdoors should\nplan for these uncomfortable conditions. Highs are anticipated\nto top out generally in the 30s to around 40 degrees. \n\nTemperatures trend warmer midweek into the weekend as ridging\nreturns with a slight southwest to northeast angle for the\nheight contours aloft supporting some relative warm air\nadvection. Temperatures will likely warm back to the upper 40s\nto low 50s by the end of the week, running 5 to 10 degrees above\nseasonal averages. \n\nModel consensus continues to favor a mature system to approach\nthe region by the end of the week into the weekend. GFS and NBM\nsolutions have trended towards the ECMWF solution with a later\nonset of precipitation with the front approach sometime Friday\ninto Saturday. Model difference continue for a preceding\ntrough, however, with some supporting lower chances of showers \nThursday as a trough moves through Canada. Depending on system \nevolution, winds may be gusty around the low as it moves through\nwith some models pointing to potential for isallobaric winds \nadding to gradient speeds. However, they agree on the initial \nprecipitation phase being rain with some potential for backside \ncooling that could result in cooling profiles and potentially a\nlittle snowfall - still too early to tease out details at this \ntime.",
